Q: Locally running API and web app: no &#39;Access-Control-Allow-Origin&#39; header is present on the requested resource A: <p>Your CORS policy name doesn't match between <code>AddPolicy</code>, <code>UseCors</code> and <code>RequireCors</code>, and <code>WithOrigins</code> uses the wrong value. The browser sends the request from <strong><code>https://localhost:7000</code></strong> (see the exact error message). You told ASP.NET Core to allow <code>http://192.168.1.169</code>, which is not the origin.</p>
<p>Here is the minimal, correct fix for <code>Program.cs</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

builder.Services.AddCors(options =&gt;
{
    options.AddPolicy(&quot;AllowLocalFrontend&quot;, policy =&gt;
    {
        policy
            .WithOrigins(&quot;https://localhost:7000&quot;)   // ← exact origin from the error
            .AllowAnyHeader()
            .AllowAnyMethod()
            .AllowCredentials();                     // remove if you don't need cookies/auth
    });
});

var app = builder.Build();

app.UseStaticFiles();
app.UseRouting();

// Important: CORS middleware must be after UseRouting and before UseAuthorization
app.UseCors(&quot;AllowLocalFrontend&quot;);

app.UseAuthorization();

app.MapControllers();                    // no .RequireCors() needed when using global policy
app.MapHub&lt;DatabaseHub&gt;(&quot;/databaseHub&quot;);

var dbService = app.Services.GetRequiredService&lt;DatabaseService&gt;();
_ = Task.Run(() =&gt; dbService.PollForUpdates());

app.Run();
</code></pre>
<h3>Why this works</h3>
<ul>
<li><code>WithOrigins(&quot;https://localhost:7000&quot;)</code> exactly matches the browser's <code>Origin</code> header.</li>
<li>Policy name is now consistent everywhere.</li>
<li><code>UseCors(&quot;AllowLocalFrontend&quot;)</code> applies the policy to <strong>all</strong> endpoints (including preflight <code>OPTIONS</code> requests that <code>fetch</code> with <code>Content-Type: application/json</code> triggers).</li>
</ul>

Q: chakra UI - vertical alignment at top A: <p>I recently ran in to this and this answer came up. Even though it seems very old, I will add what worked for me for future reference:</p>
<p>Try converting the HStack to a Flex component. Here's an example:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;Flex minHeight=&quot;100vh&quot; bg={&quot;blue.700&quot;}&gt;
  &lt;VStack spacing={2} bg={&quot;red.200&quot;}&gt;
    &lt;Text&gt;test2&lt;/Text&gt;
    &lt;Text&gt;test3&lt;/Text&gt;
  &lt;/VStack&gt;

  &lt;VStack bg={&quot;green.200&quot;}&gt;
    {/* {showPieces()} */}
    &lt;Text&gt;test2&lt;/Text&gt;
    &lt;Text&gt;test3&lt;/Text&gt;
  &lt;/VStack&gt;
&lt;/Flex&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Hopefully that helps in the future</p>
